1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,762
Your website's code hasn't even
started.

2
00:00:03,012 --> 00:00:06,433
But PHP is already parsing an
incoming request.

3
00:00:06,683 --> 00:00:10,793
While internal data is still
being initialized, a memory

4
00:00:10,793 --> 00:00:11,706
allocation fails.

5
00:00:11,956 --> 00:00:16,005
PHP's old out-of-memory handling
jumps out of that work.

6
00:00:16,555 --> 00:00:19,921
The trouble comes next: cleanup
can treat the unfinished

7
00:00:19,921 --> 00:00:21,043
data as valid.

8
00:00:21,293 --> 00:00:25,065
With a carefully crafted
request, that memory error can

9
00:00:25,065 --> 00:00:27,999
become remote code execution,
without a login.

10
00:00:28,249 --> 00:00:31,815
The code runs with the web
worker's permissions, so

11
00:00:31,815 --> 00:00:33,400
root access isn't automatic.

12
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:37,477
This was demonstrated with
Apache's mod_php; other

13
00:00:37,477 --> 00:00:39,664
deployments have different
conditions.

14
00:00:39,914 --> 00:00:42,888
PHP patched the flaw on October
twenty-first.

15
00:00:43,138 --> 00:00:46,829
Update the PHP runtime, then
verify the running service

16
00:00:46,829 --> 00:00:48,470
has loaded the fix.

17
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:51,944
A firewall can't repair the
runtime underneath your site.
